edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459198 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f941bfd8-147c-56b4-bdae-a7b1d8dbf539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459207 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459207 #edb/ref 1069 "q-316298" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459207 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459207 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the average value ${g}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $g(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ on the interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{6}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459207 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ef347b8-64ff-5465-9d5f-8f167a76e412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459207 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{6}{\\pi }(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }[(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}))-(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\cdot 0)+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }[(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(0+0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }[(\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1)-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }(\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{16}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459215 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459215 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459215 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459215 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for integrals for  $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+10$ on the interval $[-2,4].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459215 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "212286d9-e641-57e0-939d-5fc079361cc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459215 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-2)}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}2{x}^{2}+10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+10x)|}_{-2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(\\frac{2\\cdot {4}^{3}}{3}+10\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)^{3}}{3}+10\\cdot (-2))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 108 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =18 \\\\ 2{c}^{2}+10 & =18 \\\\ {c}^{2} & =4 \\\\ c & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459216 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459216 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459216 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459216 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/97/97b55bafaa70ef2984575a30f06209e11304ff975df689833134fa672fdd7183.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x),$ which is defined on $[-5,4],$ is shown above. The areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$, $7$ and $3,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the average value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,4].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "213c9893-b1bb-52cc-a602-24f43ba80406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459216 #edb/ref 1074 "The average value of a function on a given interval is\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, we see that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-5+7-3=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, applying the average value formula, we find that the average value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-5)}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459219 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459219 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459219 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ on the interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459219 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "280ecae1-be4d-57a7-89a2-e0f1aff5f58a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459219 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{3}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459230 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459230 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459230 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459230 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the numbers $c$ such that the average value of $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x-10$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d2abb2d-5fae-5b42-8b46-286ab0af6f29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459230 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}{\\int }_{0}^{c}(3{x}^{2}-6x-10)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 8 & ={\\frac{1}{c}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-10x)|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{1}{c}[({c}^{3}-3{c}^{2}-10c)-0] \\\\ 8 & ={c}^{2}-3c-10 \\\\ 0 & ={c}^{2}-3c-18 \\\\ 0 & =(c+3)(c-6) \\\\ c & =-3,\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $c>0,$ we conclude that $c=6$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459232 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459232 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459232 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459232 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459232 #edb/ref 1071 "In **ascending order**, the **exact** values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for integrals for  $f(x)={x}^{2}-4x$ on the interval $[0,3]$ are\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,c=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459232 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4549174b-8bf8-5249-ad67-6ec2c93dbb2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459232 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-0}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}-4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2})|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-2\\cdot {3}^{2})-(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-2\\cdot {0}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [(9-18)-(0-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =-3 \\\\ {c}^{2}-4c & =-3 \\\\ {c}^{2}-4c+3 & =0 \\\\ (c-2)^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ c-2 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ c & =1,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem on the interval $[0,3]$ are \n\n$$\nc=1,\\,\\text{and}\\,c=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459242 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459242 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459242 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459242 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for integrals for  $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ on the interval $[0,4].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459242 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5dd04ec5-fe77-51e6-b7d8-942237bfd483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459242 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-0}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ c & =\\frac{16}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459251 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459251 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459251 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459251 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the numbers $c$ such that the average value of $f(x)=9{x}^{2}+6x-18$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459251 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74a8d294-acf0-5af3-a3bb-298803beabab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459251 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}{\\int }_{0}^{c}(9{x}^{2}+6x-18)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 0 & ={\\frac{1}{c}(3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-18x)|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{c}[(3{c}^{3}+3{c}^{2}-18c)-0] \\\\ 0 & =3{c}^{2}+3c-18 \\\\ 0 & =(c+3)(3c-6) \\\\ c & =-3,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $c>0,$ we conclude that $c=2$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459253 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459253 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459253 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the average value of $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ on the interval $[1,8]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459253 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7616dfe0-de8d-5c42-97e8-80147a4d58bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459253 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8-1}{\\int }_{1}^{8}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{7}(\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4/3})|}_{1}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {8}^{4/3})-(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4/3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot ({8}^{1/3})^{4})-\\frac{3}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{4})-\\frac{3}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 16)-\\frac{3}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(12-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459273 #edb/ref 1069 "q-316284" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459273 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459273 #edb/ref 1071 "In **ascending order**, the **exact** values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for integrals for  $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ are\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,c=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459273 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b16fe381-16d5-5d5d-acea-e99a321f7b25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459273 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-(-3)}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}+2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{x}^{2})|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+{3}^{2})-(\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}+(-3)^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot [(9+9)-(-9+9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =3 \\\\ {c}^{2}+2c & =3 \\\\ {c}^{2}+2c-3 & =0 \\\\ (c+1)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ c+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ c & =-3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem on the interval $[-3,3]$ are \n\n$$\nc=-3,\\,\\text{and}\\,c=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459274 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459274 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459274 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459274 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ on the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459274 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2fa558e-45d2-5d75-8de8-3dcedde3e30f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459274 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for the integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(b-a)f(c) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{1-0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{1}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1))-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1))] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{c+1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ c+1 & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\ c & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459276 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459276 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459276 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459276 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459276 #edb/ref 1071 "In **ascending order**, the **exact** values of $c$ such that the average value of $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x-6$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $6$ are\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and  $\\,c=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459276 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b4a78cea-04c5-5a3c-8466-a049995b5873" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459276 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}{\\int }_{0}^{c}(3{x}^{2}-2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 6 & ={\\frac{1}{c}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-6x)|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{c}[({c}^{3}-{c}^{2}-6c)-0] \\\\ 6c & ={c}^{3}-{c}^{2}-6c \\\\ 0 & ={c}^{3}-{c}^{2}-12c \\\\ 0 & ={c}^{2}-c-12 \\\\ 0 & =(c-4)(c+3) \\\\ c & =-3,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in **ascending order**, the values of $c$ are \n\n$$\nc=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,c=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459287 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459287 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459287 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459287 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9d/9d12008688f8cded02aff352773323ce6b4b8a978746b720ebcba319b7b71598.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x),$ which is defined on $[-5,5],$ is shown above. It consists of line segments.\n\nFind the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of $f(x)$ on $[-4,4].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459287 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cdb68157-b846-5747-a6b0-cb5c936a1bd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459287 #edb/ref 1074 "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-4-6 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-4)}\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459291 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459291 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459291 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cc/cc31e5e85263f5a08a10c73c76cf5ba5eec2c27195159da31ef96a614efba7ed.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x),$ which is defined on $[-2,4],$ is shown above. It consists of three line segments. Find the average value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,4].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4039708-ee8a-57dd-b312-f84f190dda62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459291 #edb/ref 1074 "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-2)}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459304 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459304 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459304 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459304 #edb/ref 1071 "The **exact** value of $c$ such that the average value of $f(x)=9\\sqrt{x}+2$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $11$ is\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459304 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5043abb-6077-57ce-a472-1083e29053c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459304 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 11 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}{\\int }_{0}^{c}(9\\sqrt{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 11 & ={\\frac{1}{c}(6{x}^{3/2}+2x)|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 11 & =\\frac{1}{c}[(6{c}^{3/2}+2c)-0] \\\\ 11 & =6\\sqrt{c}+2 \\\\ 9 & =6\\sqrt{c} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\sqrt{c} \\\\ c & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $c$ is \n\n$$\nc=\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
